  "details": "<p>The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Macedonian fortress above Anchiale.\u00a0<span>Two locations have been tentatively suggested for Kundu (classical\u00a0Kyinda): (1) modern\u00a0</span><a class=\"external-link\" href=\"../658378\" target=\"_self\" title=\"\">Anavarza</a><span>, ca. twenty km southeast of Kozan (=\u00a0Siss\u00fb), and (2) modern Tirmil Tepe, ca. two km northeast of\u00a0</span><a class=\"external-link\" href=\"../648555\" target=\"_self\" title=\"\">Mersin</a><span>. The former seems more plausible given\u00a0that Kundu and Siss\u00fb mentioned together in Esarhaddon's inscriptions, although this cannot be proven with certainty. Note that\u00a0</span><span>Anavarza is generally associated with classical\u00a0<a class=\"external-link\" href=\"../658378\" target=\"_self\" title=\"\">Anazarbos/Caesarea/Ioustin(ian)oupolis</a>.\u00a0</span><span>Kundu is not in the vicinity of\u00a0</span><a class=\"external-link\" href=\"../678393\" target=\"_self\" title=\"\">Sidon</a><span>, as suggested by W. Mayer in 1995.\u00a0</span></p>",
  "review_state": "published",
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "description": "Kundu is mentioned in Akkadian royal inscriptions of the seventh-century-BC Assyrian king Esarhaddon. In Classical times, Strabo describes Kyinda as a fortress where Macedonian kings deposited treasure. Eumenes reportedly captured the treasure when he revolted against Antigonus. The site is now unlocated.",
